Do these three changes only. They should deliver most of the available CPU savings.

Never send per-connection events to enforcement

Aggregate activity by IP for one second, then send one decision per IP. At 1M connections/s and 100k IPs, this can reduce userspace operations from roughly 1M/s to at most 100k/s, often much lower.

``rust
use std::{collections::HashMap, net::IpAddr};

let mut counts: HashMap<IpAddr, u32> = HashMap::withcapacity(100000);
let mut interval = tokio::time::interval(std::time::Duration::fromsecs(1));
interval.setmissedtickbehavior(tokio::time::MissedTickBehavior::Skip);

loop {
    tokio::select! {
        Some(ip) = ingressrx.recv() => {
            *counts.entry(ip).ordefault() += 1;
        }

         = interval.tick() => {
            for (ip, count) in counts.drain() {
                if count >= BLOCKTHRESHOLD {
                    let  = enforcetx.trysend(Command::Block(ip));
                }
            }
        }

        else => break,
    }
}
`

Better still, keep packet/connection counting in eBPF and read counters once per second.

Coalesce block operations and update XDP only on state changes

Make the enforcement worker own a HashSet. Duplicate blocks and unblocks then cost only a set lookup and cause no XDP syscall.

`rust
use std::collections::HashSet;
use std::net::IpAddr;

let mut blocked = HashSet::withcapacity(100000);

while let Some(command) = enforcerx.recv().await {
    match command {
        Command::Block(ip) if blocked.insert(ip) => {
            xdp.applyblock(ip)?;
            store.saveblock(ip)?;
        }

        Command::Unblock(ip) if blocked.remove(&ip) => {
            xdp.removeblock(ip)?;
            store.removeblock(ip)?;
        }

        Command::Block() | Command::Unblock() => {
            // Already in the requested state: do nothing.
        }
    }
}
`

Use a bounded channel:

`rust
let (enforcetx, enforcerx) = tokio::sync::mpsc::channel(4096);
`

Do not use DashMap inside this single-owner worker. A regular HashSet or HashMap avoids locking and is cheaper.

Stop running full XDP synchronization every second

Use individual inserts/removes during normal operation:

`rust
xdp.applyblock(ip)?;
xdp.removeblock(ip)?;
`

Run sync_blocklist() only:

• At startup
• After reattaching XDP
• After an XDP update failure

Do not iterate over all 100k blocked IPs during every one-second flush.

Minimal target design

`text
Ingress
   -> aggregate counts by IP for 1 second
   -> bounded enforcement channel
   -> one enforcement worker with HashSet
   -> XDP insert/remove only when state changes
``

Skip compiler tuning, IPC conversion, custom allocators, timer wheels, and Tokio worker tuning for now. The highest-gain quick fix is simply:

> Aggregate before the channel, deduplicate in one worker, and never perform full XDP syncs during steady-state operation.
